---
import LabLayout from '../../layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { MATERIALS, MODEL, PAYLOAD_LIMIT_T } from '../../simulation/engine';
const sections = [['scope','Model scope'],['flight-school','Missions & studies'],['materials','Materials & sizing'],['recovery','Recovery modes'],['electrodynamic','Electrical experiment'],['events','Capture & release'],['equations','Equations'],['limits','Success & limits'],['validation','Tests'],['sources','Sources & display']];
---
<LabLayout title="Method — Tether Lab" description="A readable guide to the Tether Lab model: materials, capture and release, recovery, equations, tests and limitations." active="method">
  <main class="guide" id="lab-content">
    <header class="guide-header">
      <p class="micro">THE MODEL GUIDE</p><h1>Know what you’re flying.</h1>
      <p>The globe shows a calculation, not a scripted success story. This guide explains what the model includes, which assumptions you control, and where its answers stop.</p>
      <div class="guide-actions"><a class="button-link" href="/lab/">Back to the workbench →</a><a href="/lab/architectures/">Explore tether architectures</a></div>
      <div class="guide-meta"><span>{MODEL}</span><span>Planar rigid-body dynamics</span><span>Educational, not flight-qualified</span></div>
    </header>
    <div class="guide-layout">
      <nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span>IN THIS GUIDE</span>{sections.map(([id,title])=><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a>)}</nav>
      <div class="guide-body">
        <section class="guide-section" id="scope">
          <h2>One Earth. One rotating facility.</h2>
          <p>The current experiment is a symmetric, single-stage rotovator. Gravity acts on the distributed tether mass, its hub and its terminal modules. Translation and rotation evolve together.</p>
          <div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Calculated in this build</h3><ul><li>Distributed spherical-Earth gravity and torque</li><li>Material-dependent mass and rotational inertia</li><li>Ideal matched capture and impulse-free release</li><li>Axial-load and full-segment clearance checks</li><li>Coast, bounded chemical or E0 electrodynamic recovery</li></ul></div><div class="scope-box omitted"><h3>Not simulated</h3><ul><li>Atmospheric flight or capture guidance</li><li>Cable waves, elasticity, fatigue or creep</li><li>Debris impacts, severing or repairs</li><li>Plasma collection, thermal dynamics or inbound-traffic recovery</li><li>T4 joints, lunar encounters or solar-system dynamics</li></ul></div></div>
          <div class="guide-note"><p>A successful run means the modeled scenario met its conditions. It does not establish that a real tether could be built or flown.</p></div>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="flight-school">
          <h2>Learn from the next flight.</h2>
          <p><strong>Flight school and the sandbox use the same equations.</strong> Each challenge supplies a starting design and states which controls can change. The objectives include two qualifying deliveries, a minimum apogee, a dry-mass limit and a loaded-propellant budget. Escape trajectories do not satisfy these bound-orbit missions.</p>
          <p>Checks use the complete calculated run, not the current replay position. A passed mission is stored only in this browser; it is not a public leaderboard or hardware certification. Unrun edits never update the result of an earlier flight.</p>
          <div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Guided replay</h3><p>Pause at actual capture, release and recovery events. These checkpoints explain the recorded state without changing it. Playback speed affects the view, not the numerical time step.</p></div><div class="scope-box"><h3>Trade studies</h3><p>Run separate simulations for recovery, material, release-phase or payload variants. Selecting a result restores that exact design and its recorded flight. Incomplete and model-limited runs remain visible.</p></div></div>
          <p>Pin a flight before changing a design to retain a comparison baseline. Fuel, mass and clearance differences are observations, not an automatic efficiency ranking: runs can deliver different energy or last for different durations. Coast also removes the carried propellant mass.</p>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Read the trade plot and structural inspector</summary><div><p>The trade plot shows simulated duration against the first payload’s specific mechanical-energy gain. Filled circles complete both deliveries; open circles do not. A cross means there was no release. The result table and debrief provide the quantities behind the points.</p><p>The structural inspector samples the same axial-load cuts used by the solver’s stop condition at the selected replay time. It shows section area, tension and stress against the chosen allowable. It does not model transverse deformation or elastic waves. Cable thickness is enlarged for visibility.</p></div></details>
          <p><strong>Export flight report</strong> records the design, model version, numerical settings, events, outputs and challenge checks. This report is distinct from the small design JSON used for imports and sharing.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="materials">
          <h2>Change the material. Keep the geometry.</h2>
          <p>Material selection changes density and the strength input. It does <strong>not</strong> silently thicken the tether. That makes the trade visible: the same dimensions can become lighter, heavier or unable to carry the modeled load.</p>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap"><table><caption class="micro">STRUCTURAL INPUTS</caption><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Cross-section</b></td><td>Center area in mm². Uniform keeps that area throughout. The parabolic taper ends at 30% of center area.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Allowable stress</b></td><td>Ultimate fiber strength divided by the chosen safety factor. This simplified derating excludes environmental and joint losses.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Resize</b></td><td>An explicit calculation for the lower-radial load with a 15% area reserve. It may refuse a design. A resized design still needs a mission run.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Payload range</b></td><td>Standard: 0.1–20 t. Extended: 0.1–{PAYLOAD_LIMIT_T} t. Both use the same equations; the upper input limit is not a rated capacity.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Inspect the material profiles</summary><div>{MATERIALS.map(m=><article class="source-record"><span class="micro">{m.source?'PUBLISHED FIBER DATA':'HYPOTHETICAL INPUT'}</span><h3>{m.name}</h3><p>{m.basis}. {m.id!=='custom'&&`${m.density} kg/m³; ${(m.ultimate/1e9).toFixed(1)} GPa ultimate strength.`}</p><p>{m.locator}</p>{m.source&&<a href={m.source}>Read the source ↗</a>}</article>)}<p>Kevlar 49 uses the 3.0 GPa conditioned-yarn value, not the resin-impregnated-strand value. “Future carbon” is a scenario assumption, not a claim of available hardware. No endorsement is implied.</p></div></details>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="recovery">
          <h2>No free reset for the orbit.</h2>
          <div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Chemical recovery</h3><p>The facility carries the selected propellant mass. A bounded controller uses thrust and torque to recover orbit and spin. Fuel is consumed; empty tanks stop thrust.</p></div><div class="scope-box"><h3>Coast</h3><p>No active recovery and no onboard propellant. The orbit evolves after the transfer. The same readiness conditions still govern the next handoff.</p></div></div>
          <p>Switching back to Chemical restores the last budget entered in this session. That remembered setting is not carried as hidden mass in Coast. Telemetry always belongs to the last completed calculation, not an unrun edit.</p>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Controller assumptions and fuel accounting</summary><div><p>Ideal steerable thrusters produce net translation and a terminal couple. A simple feedback law seeks the initial circular radius and angular momentum. Spin follows a circular short-rod gravity-gradient reference; actual propagation still uses distributed gravity.</p><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0" aria-label="Propellant-flow equation">mass flow = (|translation thrust| + |torque| / half-span) / (Isp × g₀)</pre><p>The total force budget is bounded. The reported fuel cost belongs to this controller, not an optimized minimum. Feed-system mass, plume interactions and transient cable loads are omitted.</p></div></details>
          <p><strong>Electrodynamic / E0</strong> exchanges momentum through a specified field and plasma circuit. No chemical fuel is loaded. Its conductor and user-budgeted power hardware add mass; current, voltage and electrical power cap the calculated force. Read the separate E0 scope below. <a href="/lab/architectures/#recovery">Incoming-traffic exchange</a> is not simulated yet.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="electrodynamic">
          <h2>Trade propellant for power. Keep the bill visible.</h2>
          <p>E0 adds two independently driven, insulated conductor segments along the outer part of the rotating arms. Each circuit is assumed to close through ambient plasma. Its Lorentz force is perpendicular to the local cable; the controller cannot ask for an arbitrary thrust direction. Separately controlled currents also produce a moment about the center of mass.</p>
          <div class="guide-note"><p><strong>This is an actuator experiment, not a plasma-performance prediction.</strong> The orbit is equatorial in an aligned dipole field. The plasma is assumed to corotate with Earth. Circuit current is a user-specified ceiling, not a demonstrated collection capability at that altitude. <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20070013898">NASA’s MXER current-collection analysis</a> explains why actual collection is a mission-specific design problem.</p></div>
          <p>Choose Electrodynamic in Recovery and load the powered-conductor example: 200 km span, 700 km initial altitude and 0.5 t per shipment. Pin a run and compare bus-power caps. Raising power need not improve the result when current or voltage is limiting. A zero-power variant keeps the same conductor and hardware mass but opens the circuits; selecting Coast instead removes that electrical system.</p>
          <div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Counted</h3><p>Distributed conductor mass, Lorentz force and torque, back EMF from relative motion, resistive loss, contact loss, converter loss, dumped generated energy, and caps on drive voltage, current and input power.</p></div><div class="scope-box omitted"><h3>Not predicted</h3><p>Electron collection, cathode consumables, plasma density, sheath physics, geomagnetic tilt, inclination, eclipse, batteries, temperature, radiators, insulation failure, current rise time, flexible response or a qualified power-system mass.</p></div></div>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Field, circuit and energy equations</summary><div><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0" aria-label="Electrodynamic equations">Bz = −30 μT × (Earth radius / local radius)³
F = ∫ I (u × B) ds
τ = ∫ (r − R) × dF
v_plasma = Ω_Earth × r
k = ∫ (u × B) · (v − v_plasma) ds
U = R I + k + sign(I) × 100 V
P_terminal = U I
P_bus = max(P_terminal, 0) / 0.9
P_dump = max(−P_terminal, 0)
P_bus + P_environment = P_mechanical + P_losses</pre><p>The field magnitude, 100 V combined contact drop and 90% conversion efficiency are declared scenario assumptions. Generated electricity is dissipated, never silently stored or used to refill a tank. Environment exchange uses the same local force and corotating-plasma velocity as the EMF calculation. Signed mechanical work includes both orbital and rotational work; it is not simply payload energy gain.</p><p>Conductor density is modeled at 2,700 kg/m³ and fixed resistivity at 2.82 × 10⁻⁸ Ω·m. These are ideal aluminum-like reference properties, not a selected alloy or temperature solution. <a href="https://www.cleanroom.byu.edu/Resistivities">BYU’s resistivity reference</a> gives the latter value at 20°C. The structural material remains separate: aluminum mass is added without crediting it with strength.</p><p>The voltage limiter conservatively opens a circuit when the back EMF plus contact allowance exceeds the drive cap. Safe open-circuit insulation is not established. A controller may dissipate energy while correcting the state; it is neither power-optimal nor guaranteed to restore readiness in six hours.</p></div></details>
          <p>Power/contact hardware mass is your explicit budget, carried at the hub. The lab does not infer a credible solar-array, battery or radiator mass from power. Automatic radial resizing is disabled for E0 because that shortcut does not include conductor loads; the full trajectory still checks the chosen strength-tether section.</p>
          <p>The recorder shows the current replay state. The full-run debrief and JSON report separately total electrical input, signed mechanical work, environment exchange and losses. None of those totals are taken from unrun control changes.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="events">
          <h2>The payload gets the tip’s actual velocity.</h2>
          <p>The first incoming orbit is constructed to meet the tip with matching position and velocity. The lead-in is propagated, but the lab does not solve vehicle guidance or capture shock.</p>
          <p>Capture changes the combined center of mass without teleporting existing material points. Release adds no ejector impulse: the payload departs with its current position and velocity and is propagated separately.</p>
          <p><strong>Release phase is Earth-relative.</strong> It is measured against the moving local vertical, not a fixed half-turn in inertial space. Both orbit and tether rotation matter.</p>
          <p>Payload 2 is a separate shipment, not a recapture of Payload 1. Once the facility is ready, a coast forecast checks the next lower radial pass. If that forecast passes, the lab constructs an incoming state by propagating backward from the predicted meeting, then propagates that particle independently forward. The final approach is up to 90 seconds long; shorter windows are reported as such. Both captures must match within 2 m and 0.02 m/s, and the second also rechecks facility readiness. Failed checks do not attach a payload. These are numerical tolerances for constructed examples, not hardware capture-envelope requirements. Launch, autonomous guidance and return-traffic planning are not simulated.</p>
          <p><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20060051892">NASA’s MXER dynamics study</a> discusses why high-precision prediction matters for tether capture. It is background research, not validation of this simplified rendezvous model.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="equations">
          <h2>Small equations, explicit assumptions.</h2>
          <p>The rigid body uses 48 tether mass cells, a 30 t hub and two 2 t terminal modules. Density and area set the distributed mass. Propellant, when selected, is at the hub. The numerical core uses metres, kilograms, seconds and radians.</p>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Inspect the translation and rotation equations</summary><div><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0" aria-label="Rigid-body equations">rᵢ = R + qᵢ [cos θ, sin θ]
vᵢ = V + ω qᵢ [−sin θ, cos θ]
Fᵢ = −μ mᵢ rᵢ / |rᵢ|³
M R̈ = Σ Fᵢ + thrust
I θ̈ = Σ (rᵢ − R) × Fᵢ + thrust torque</pre><p>R and V locate the instantaneous center of mass. qᵢ is a material point’s distance along the tether from that center. θ is inertial orientation and ω is spin rate.</p><p>RK4 advances the state with a default 2 s step. Capture, release, radial-pass and limit events split the time step. Playback speed does not change the integration step.</p></div></details>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="limits">
          <h2>Success is a condition, not a score.</h2>
          <p>Both payloads must leave with greater Earth-relative mechanical energy and an osculating perigee above 120 km. Before the second capture, the facility must meet the following readiness conditions for 60 s and satisfy them again at the next eligible pass.</p>
          <div class="guide-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Readiness check</th><th>Tolerance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Radius relative to target</td><td>15 km</td></tr><tr><td>Radial speed</td><td>8 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Tangential speed relative to target</td><td>12 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Spin relative to reference</td><td>0.5%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p>A run stops when any part of the tether crosses the 120 km model cutoff, axial stress exceeds the assumed allowable, or a section requires a compressive force beyond the numerical tolerance. These are model limits, not animated explosions or predictions of survivable re-entry.</p>
          <p>The scenario has a six-hour integration budget. Failure to make the second delivery within that budget is not proof that no possible controller or mission schedule could succeed.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="validation">
          <h2>Reproducible, not self-certifying.</h2>
          <p>Tests cover challenge pass/fail criteria and reachable solutions, fixed-variable trade studies, agreement between the load inspector and solver, mass and inertia, point continuity, capture/release conservation, conservative coast, interior-segment clearance, material limits, finite fuel, input bounds, versioned imports, continuous event interpolation, separate shipment identities, both numerical rendezvous checks and time-step / mass-cell convergence. A separate SciPy DOP853 calculation supplies a conservative-coast reference.</p>
          <p>The expanded payload range has finite-state and limit-handling regression coverage. It is not a validated engineering envelope for every combination of inputs.</p>
          <p><a href="https://github.com/RelyOnFire/skyhook-industries/tree/tether-lab-first-playable/tests">Read the tests ↗</a></p>
          <details class="guide-details"><summary>Saved designs and the 0.4 update</summary><div><p>Configuration schema 2 records the architecture explicitly and supports payload inputs up to 250 t. Model {MODEL} adds E0 electrodynamics, conductor mass and an electrical energy ledger. Both incoming approaches remain independently propagated with persistent shipment identity. Coast still carries zero propellant. Older 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 designs require explicit review before recalculation in this model; prior results are not reused or relabeled.</p><p>Share URLs contain bounded JSON inputs in their fragment. Imports are validated data, not executable code. Local saves use browser storage. Export JSON for a portable copy.</p></div></details>
        </section>
        <section class="guide-section" id="sources">
          <h2>Track the object, not just an orange dot.</h2>
          <p>Globe, payload-follow and orbital-plane views read the same trajectory. Choose Facility, Payload 1 or Payload 2 in the object list, then select Follow. That identity remains selected through later captures and releases. Payload 1 stays orange; Payload 2 stays violet with a diamond marker. Both retain numbered labels in the globe and orbital-plane views. Before a selected shipment’s recorded approach begins, no substitute object is followed. Follow also works in the 2D fallback; approaching or attached payloads open at a closer scale. Marker size is a screen aid, not a physical spacecraft dimension. Velocity arrows use a shared display scale within each view; they are not force arrows. Cable width, markers and the visual atmosphere are enlarged. Sunlight is illustrative. Chemical and Coast retain a 28° illustrative display tilt, not a modeled inclination. E0 is drawn in the equatorial plane required by its magnetic-field approximation. Green outer cable sections show the two conductors; their thickness is exaggerated. Electrical recovery has no rocket plume.</p>
          <p>Earth’s outline uses simplified, public-domain Natural Earth coastlines, without country borders. The colors are procedural illustration, not satellite photography or terrain data. <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/">Data terms ↗</a></p>
          <p>The <a href="/lab/architectures/">architecture catalogue</a> links to historical primary studies, including HASTOL, Tillotson’s T4 and MXER. Those studies describe other systems; their results are not passed off as validation of this lab.</p>
          <p>The older project sizing studies remain in the <a href="/reference-architecture/">reference archive</a>. Their estimates are not used as proof that a lab mission will work.</p>
          <a class="button-link" href="/lab/">Back to your experiment →</a>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</LabLayout>
